{"author":{"id":"673e1e1b6fccff4da5524407c8a8cdc7caf18221aabb93f2bb36ffef5c2a95e8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779773220,"nonce":"6znSYNTto9TdcKlSX4zMLgrl1sg=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOSn4-Gc8AAAABDYJgxw","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/karpenter-provider-rackspace-spot/issues/8","origin":"github"},"title":"Embedded pricing snapshot + scheduled refresh workflow","message":"**Post-MVP item. Pattern reference: [cloudpilot-ai/karpenter-provider-gcp/.github/workflows/update-pricing.yaml](https://github.com/cloudpilot-ai/karpenter-provider-gcp/blob/main/.github/workflows/update-pricing.yaml), which mirrors [aws/karpenter-provider-aws/.github/workflows/codegen.yaml](https://github.com/aws/karpenter-provider-aws/blob/main/.github/workflows/codegen.yaml).**\n\nToday the pricing provider (`pkg/providers/pricing`) reads `ServerClass.CurrentMarketPricePerHour` from the live Rackspace API at scheduling time. That's functional but fragile:\n\n- Scheduling decisions pay a network round-trip.\n- If `spot.rackspace.com` is degraded, the Karpenter scheduler has no pricing to bin-pack with.\n\nThe convention in this ecosystem is to embed a pricing snapshot in the binary as a fallback floor, and refresh it periodically via a scheduled workflow.\n\n### Proposed\n\n1. **Embed**: add `pkg/providers/pricing/initial-prices.json` and load it via `//go:embed`. Schema: `{serverClassName: {region, onDemandPricePerHour, marketPricePercentile50, ...}}`. Source it from the public S3 percentile feed at `https://ngpc-prod-public-data.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/percentiles.json` — no credentials needed (simpler than CloudPilot's GCP setup, which has to auth into a billing API).\n2. **Refresh**: `.github/workflows/update-pricing.yml` runs weekly (cron `0 6 * * 1`) and on `workflow_dispatch`. Downloads the percentile feed, regenerates `initial-prices.json`, opens a PR via `peter-evans/create-pull-request@v8` labeled `pricing,automated`.\n3. **Runtime layering**: keep live API as the authoritative source; embedded JSON is fallback only when the API errors out or for cold-start before the first refresh completes.\n\n### Not blocked by, but related to\n\n- #4 (rate-limit / quota handling) — embedded prices reduce per-decision API calls, which helps rate-limiting too. But the motivation here is *availability*, not throttling.","files":null}]}